setup-stopped = { $script } stopped with status { $status }; nothing after the failed step was done
setup-not-with-sudo = run without sudo or doas: the build needs no root, the script asks for it when installing
setup-root-needed = the installation steps need root: install sudo or doas, or run this script as root
setup-no-account-tool = cannot create the { $account } account: no systemd-sysusers, useradd or adduser
setup-bad-manager = ELANFP_SERVICE_MANAGER must be systemd, openrc, runit, dinit, s6 or none
setup-bad-bridge = ELANFP_FPRINTD_BRIDGE must be yes or no
setup-bad-notify = ELANFP_NOTIFY must be yes or no
setup-bad-pcrs = ELANFP_TPM2_PCRS must look like 7 or 7+11
setup-no-cargo = cargo is required to build the driver
setup-pcrs-need-tpm = ELANFP_TPM2_PCRS needs systemd-creds and a TPM 2.0
setup-no-artifact = build output { $path } is missing
setup-key-lost = templates exist in { $state } but the key { $key } is gone: they cannot be decrypted any more
setup-key-lost-advice = restore the key from a backup, or remove the templates with uninstall.sh --purge and enroll again
setup-service-failed = the elanfpd service did not start under { $manager }: check its log
setup-manager = service manager:  { $manager }
setup-key-tpm = template key:     { $path }, sealed with the TPM and the host key, not bound to PCRs
setup-key-tpm-kept = template key:     { $path }, sealed with the TPM and the host key by an earlier installation; this run did not change it
setup-key-pcrs-kept = the binding to PCR { $pcrs } was not applied: the key already exists, and only a new key can be bound. Remove the key together with the templates (uninstall.sh --purge), install again with ELANFP_TPM2_PCRS and enroll the fingers again
setup-key-not-created = could not create the key { $path }; an existing file, if there is one, was not touched
setup-elevation-failed = a check as root could not be made (sudo or doas refused): nothing was changed, run the installation again
setup-key-tpm-pcrs = template key:     { $path }, sealed with the TPM and the host key, bound to PCR { $pcrs }
setup-key-file = template key:     { $path }, a private file of the service (no TPM sealing here)
setup-disk-yes = disk encryption:  yes: a stolen disk or laptop does not expose the templates
setup-disk-no-tpm = disk encryption:  no: a removed disk is useless, but whoever boots this laptop from other media can unseal the key
setup-disk-no-file = disk encryption:  no: whoever reads this disk offline gets the key and the templates; encrypt the disk to close this
setup-disk-unknown = disk encryption:  not determined
setup-access-manual = device access:    no udev here: give the group elanfp read and write access to the reader's node under /dev/bus/usb
setup-access-reboot = device access:    the udev rule is installed but udev is not running: it applies after the next boot
setup-access-udev = device access:    udev rule for the built-in reader
setup-service-s6 = service:          definitions installed to { $path }: add them to your s6-rc database or link them into the scan directory
setup-service-runit = service:          { $path } is installed: link it into your runsvdir directory
setup-service-none = service:          no known service manager: start { $program } as the user elanfp with { $runtime } (0755) and { $state } (0700) owned by it
setup-service-running = service:          running
setup-bridge-yes = desktop bridge:   running: desktop settings and pam_fprintd talk to this driver through net.reactivated.Fprint
setup-bridge-manual = desktop bridge:   installed: start { $program } as the user elanfp-fprint after the service
setup-bridge-failed = desktop bridge:   installed but not running: check its log; the driver itself works without it
setup-bridge-absent = desktop bridge:   skipped: there is no D-Bus system bus on this machine
setup-bridge-no = desktop bridge:   not installed (ELANFP_FPRINTD_BRIDGE=no)
setup-bridge-no-polkit = polkit was not found: the bridge answers only requests from root (pam_fprintd in sudo and at the login screen); the fingerprint page of the desktop settings will not work
setup-bridge-name-open = WARNING: the D-Bus rules of this machine let root own the name net.reactivated.Fprint: the policy of the stock fprintd lies next to ours. Remove the fprintd package or its file from /etc/dbus-1/system.d
setup-notify-yes = notices:          when a program running as root asks for a fingerprint, a notification appears on the desktop; it starts with the next login (or run { $program } now)
setup-notify-no = notices:          not installed (ELANFP_NOTIFY=no)
setup-dev-rule = WARNING: { $path } gives every program in your session raw access to the reader.
setup-dev-rule-advice = It is only needed for QEMU passthrough and dataset collection; remove it for daily use.
setup-next = Next:
setup-next-enroll = 1. enroll a finger: { $elevate } elanfp enroll right-index-finger (or in your desktop's fingerprint settings)
setup-next-try = 2. try it: elanfp verify
setup-next-pam = 3. enable for { $elevate }: add this line as the first auth line of { $file }, and keep a second terminal with a root shell open until { $elevate } works again:
setup-no-logind = No logind library (systemd or elogind) was found: the PAM module cannot tell who sits at the console and stays closed.
setup-no-logind-advice = On a machine without remote logins you may append console=any to the PAM line.
remove-root-needed = root is needed: install sudo or doas, or run this script as root
remove-usage = usage: uninstall.sh [--purge | --bridge]
remove-bridge-done = the fprintd bridge is removed; the service itself and the templates are untouched
remove-pam-first = remove the pam_elanfp line from these files first:
remove-purged = templates, the key and the service accounts are deleted
remove-accounts-left = everything is deleted except the accounts: { $accounts }. This usually means their processes are still running: stop them and delete the accounts by hand (userdel or deluser)
remove-kept = templates ({ $state }), the key and the service accounts are kept; remove them with: uninstall.sh --purge
